            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Sealing



Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the bottom of the front door, and around the attic access hatch. 



I recommend outfitting electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with electrical wall plate gaskets. 



The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend replacing the attic hatch with a heavier piece of plywood with foam insulation glued to the top. The heavier door will sit down tighter on the weather-stripping. You can also consider attaching latches to keep the attic hatch closed tight.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 280 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Wall Insulation



The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation and about 1.5 inches of extruded polystyrene foam insulation. I have modelled adding another 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R10 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 390 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEAÂs rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>Ceilings (Gable)



The attic of your house was assumed to have 8 inches (R28) of blown cellulose insulation. There is still plenty of room for more insulation and the attic hatch has a plywood box around it that accommodate more insulation. I have modelled adding 8 additional inches of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R56.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R56, you could potentially save $ 230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEAÂs rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</ceilings>
                <foundation>Basements



Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with about 3 inches of polyurethane spray foam insulation. There is a small cold storage room that has an uninsulated wall and header space. I have modeled adding 3 inches of spray foam to the exterior wall to enclose the space in the heated area of the house. 



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R18) polyurethane spray foam insulation to the cold storage basement wall, you could potentially save $ 245 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.1 GJ.

</foundation>
                <windows>WINDOWS



Most of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing all windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 1105 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 32.2 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STARÂ® qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</windows>
                <doors>DOORS



You mentioned you have plans to replace or refit your front door. It was noted that there was some air leakage around the bottom and between the two doors. The energy savings would be minimal from replacing the door but it might cut down on some air leakage which would in turn save energy.



Recommendation:

For door replacements I recommend an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified door rated for climate zone 3. If the door has a window then I also recommend triple pane, argon filled, and low-e coated windows with vinyl frames. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>PROPANE



You currently have a 46 gallon hot water tank which is heated from the oil-fired boiler. This requires your boiler to run during the summer to supply hot water, and this can make the boiler run inefficiently. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your indirect hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 435 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>PROPANE BOILER



Your current oil fired boiler is from when the house was built which means it is about 16 years old. Most boilers will last about 25 to 30 years so this one still has some life left. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 2120 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 48.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. A condensing propane boiler would also be sealed combustion so it wouldnÂt require the combustion air duct which would have additional air leakage savings.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the boiler will be double that of the AEAÂs rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a boiler rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR



There is a 5 inch combustion air duct next to the boiler; this is to provide combustion air while the boiler is running. If you will be keeping the current boiler I suggest installing an electric damper to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating. See Heating section for other options for boilers.

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



There are ECM pump replacement available for hot water and cold water systems so you could replace the cold water circulation pump and the pump on the boilers system.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Nick Walker

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: nick.walker@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
